\section{A Match Is a Candidate, Not a Proof}

A source pattern can locate code that looks suitable for a refactoring. The refactoring must also establish that the replacement preserves the behavior the language promises. In Ant\'onio Menezes Leit\~ao's \textit{A Formal Pattern Language for Refactoring of Lisp Programs}, an antecedent matches a fragment and binds pattern variables, while a consequent is instantiated from those bindings. The language includes segment variables and predicates so it can express changes over lists of forms and inspect the fragments it binds. Leit\~ao also distinguishes rules for matching from templates for building code: a constructor that needs information from the environment is not necessarily a usable inverse pattern.

Warth and Piumarta's \textit{OMeta: an Object-Oriented Language for Pattern Matching} illustrates the use of one pattern notation over tokens and structured data, including tree transformations. For refactoring, matching the structured tree avoids false hits inside comments, strings, and longer identifiers. It does not eliminate semantic false positives. Two identifiers with the same spelling may denote different bindings; two identical-looking subexpressions may read mutable state at different points. Key metavariables by the appropriate category and compare resolved identity where the rule depends on identity. In a composed language, use the defining identity, not the identity of a symbol adapter that happens to expose it (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-138}).

\section{Worked Refactoring: Inline Without Capture}

Take a lexically scoped, call-by-value Lisp-like language. Consider this program, where the outer \texttt{y} has value \texttt{7}:
\begin{verbatim}
((lambda (x) (lambda (y) x)) y)
\end{verbatim}
A candidate rule matches an immediate application of a lambda and substitutes the argument into the body. Naive substitution returns \texttt{(lambda (y) y)}, which returns its own argument; the original closure returns \texttt{7} regardless of its argument. Before substituting, rename the inner binder and its bound uses to a fresh identity, then insert the reference to the outer binding. A valid result is \texttt{(lambda (z) y)}, provided \texttt{z} is fresh. A regression applies both original and refactored closures to \texttt{2}; both must return \texttt{7}. A tree-level check can assert the stronger local fact that the inserted reference still resolves to the original outer binding.

That example assumes the reference is to an immutable value and the language has no observable closure allocation identity. In general, substituting an arbitrary expression can duplicate evaluation, drop it entirely, or move it into a function that runs later. Restrict this rule to values under suitable language assumptions, or use a binding-preserving transformation that evaluates the argument once at the original point. Pombrio and Krishnamurthi's \textit{Inferring Type Rules for Syntactic Sugar} gives an analogous use of a temporary in the expansion of short-circuit disjunction: binding the left operand avoids evaluating it twice. A type comparison alone cannot detect that kind of change.

\section{Selection, Validation, and Publication}

When several rules match, a refactoring tool should choose a documented strategy or offer alternatives. A pair of inverse transformations can loop if both are installed as unconditional simplifications. Two matches in overlapping spans can each be valid on the original tree yet incompatible together. Compute candidates on a fixed revision, reject overlaps or re-evaluate after the first change, and report why a guard prevented a candidate if the user explicitly requested it.

To produce a patch, use syntax origins to keep comments and whitespace outside the edit intact. Then reparse the changed text. Re-resolve touched references and compare their identities against the rule's declared binding changes. Rechecking types and selected examples is useful but does not replace effect or evaluation-order guards. For an IDE, make the revision check part of publishing the patch: a proposed rewrite must not silently overwrite a more recent user edit. \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/29-proof-carrying-ir-and-certified-rewrite-contracts.md}{Markdown Dossier Chapter~29} applies the same distinction between a proposed rewrite and its validation to intermediate representations. For source, the validator also needs to know which token ranges and binding relationships the patch is meant to preserve.
